<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Texas</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Texas is the second-largest state of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> by area and population, in the south-central part of the country, bordering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-grande">Rio Grande</a>. A thinly settled province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-new-spain">New Spain</a> from the late 17th century and part of Mexico after 1821, it broke away in 1836 as the Republic of Texas and joined the United States in 1845. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">Cattle</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> and, from 1901, oil shaped its economy.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/texas.jpg" alt="Texas" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Paul B. Joiner · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Texas.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
